//? Title: Confidence intervals for proportions and event rates
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

Newcombe's interval combines two Wilson score intervals to estimate a
difference of proportions. A Poisson event rate uses exposure instead of a
fixed number of trials. Its exact interval inverts Poisson tails. For the
ratio of two rates, conditioning on the total event count gives a binomial
problem whose interval can be transformed back to the rate ratio.

```{cpp, id=count-intervals, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/proportion_test.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const auto difference = newcombe_difference_interval(2, 11, 8, 17);
    const auto rate = poisson_rate_interval(10, 22);
    const auto ratio = poisson_rate_ratio_interval(10, 22, 5, 35);
    std::println("| Quantity | Estimate | 95% lower | 95% upper |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Proportion difference, 2/11 minus 8/17 | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                 difference.difference, difference.lower, difference.upper);
    std::println("| Rate, 10 events in 22 exposure units | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                 *rate.estimate, rate.lower, rate.upper);
    std::println("| Rate ratio, (10/22) divided by (5/35) | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                 *ratio.estimate, ratio.lower, ratio.upper);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Quantity | Estimate | 95% lower | 95% upper |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Proportion difference, 2/11 minus 8/17 | -0.289 | -0.544 | 0.073 |
| Rate, 10 events in 22 exposure units | 0.455 | 0.218 | 0.836 |
| Rate ratio, (10/22) divided by (5/35) | 3.182 | 0.991 | 11.864 |
// result_end

> Estimate a ratio of two binomial risks

The score interval fits the two risks under each proposed ratio and keeps
the ratios that pass the two-sided score test. A zero numerator count
gives a lower bound of zero. With zero events in both groups, the data
leave the risk ratio unrestricted.

```{cpp, id=binomial-risk-ratio, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/proportion_test.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using datamunge::stats::risk_ratio_score_interval;
    const auto observed = risk_ratio_score_interval(2, 11, 8, 17);
    const auto zero = risk_ratio_score_interval(0, 20, 4, 20);
    std::println("| First risk / second risk | Estimate | 95% lower | 95% upper |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| (2/11) / (8/17) | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                 *observed.estimate, observed.lower, observed.upper);
    std::println("| (0/20) / (4/20) | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                 *zero.estimate, zero.lower, zero.upper);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| First risk / second risk | Estimate | 95% lower | 95% upper |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| (2/11) / (8/17) | 0.386 | 0.102 | 1.229 |
| (0/20) / (4/20) | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.870 |
// result_end

[Koopman’s score interval|https://doi.org/10.2307/2531405]
